---

<DocsLayout>
  <p>
    Start a receiver, point your framework's OpenTelemetry exporter at it, and run your agent. One trace becomes one
    <a href="/docs/concepts">session</a>: model-call spans become model calls with tokens and cost, and tool spans become tool calls.
  </p>

  <h2 id="start">Start the receiver</h2>
  <Code code="coding.sh collector" />
  <Code title="output" code={collectorOut} />
  <p>
    Leave it running and start your agent in another terminal. <code>coding.sh sessions</code> lists the runs and <code>coding.sh ui</code>
    opens them in the viewer. <code>coding.sh serve</code> takes OTLP on the same port and adds the viewer; run one or the other.
  </p>

  <details class="more" id="receiver">
    <summary>What the receiver accepts</summary>
    <ul>
      <li><code>POST /v1/traces</code> and <code>/v1/logs</code>, as protobuf or JSON, optionally gzip, deflate or br encoded. <code>/v1/metrics</code> is accepted and dropped.</li>
      <li>OpenTelemetry GenAI attributes (<code>gen_ai.*</code>), OpenInference, the Vercel AI SDK (<code>ai.*</code>), and the log events of Claude Code, Codex and older Gemini CLI.</li>
      <li>It binds to <code>127.0.0.1</code>. <code>--port 0</code> picks a free port. <code>--keep-raw</code> also keeps each source span.</li>
      <li>Secrets are removed before anything is stored, as for every other session.</li>
    </ul>
  </details>

  <h2 id="openai-agents">OpenAI Agents SDK</h2>
  <p>Install the SDK with the OpenInference instrumentor, and register it before the agent runs:</p>
  <Code code={agentsInstall} />
  <Code title="triage_agent.py" code={agentsPy} />
  <div class="note">
    <p>
      Don't set <code>OPENAI_AGENTS_DISABLE_TRACING</code>. It turns off the Agents SDK's tracing, so these spans stop too and nothing reaches
      coding.sh. The instrumentor already replaces OpenAI's own trace upload.
    </p>
  </div>
  <p><code>python triage_agent.py</code> prints <code>bug, viewer</code>. Then <code>coding.sh tree latest</code> shows how the run was stored:</p>
  <Code title="output" code={agentsTree} />
  <p>
    Today a run arrives as two sessions. The root session stands for the run and has no title, model or cost. The agent's work, its model
    calls, tool calls and cost, is in a child session named after the agent. <code>coding.sh sessions</code> lists only the root; add
    <code>--subagents</code> to list both, or open the child:
  </p>
  <Code code="coding.sh show otel-sub-187809985675330cbb4e3553051c023a-bd63609e80df1b97" />
  <Code title="output" code={agentsChild} />

  <h2 id="langgraph">LangChain and LangGraph</h2>
  <p>The OpenInference instrumentor records LangChain and LangGraph runs:</p>
  <Code code={graphInstall} />
  <Code title="triage_graph.py" code={graphPy} />
  <p>
    <code>coding.sh show latest</code> prints the run. The root session takes its title from the first user message and holds the tool calls.
    Each model step of the graph is a child session.
  </p>
  <Code title="output" code={graphOut} />
  <details class="more" id="langsmith">
    <summary>Send with LangSmith's OpenTelemetry exporter instead</summary>
    <p>LangSmith can export OpenTelemetry only, with no LangSmith account. Set these and run the graph without the instrumentor:</p>
    <Code code={langsmithEnv} />
    <p>A LangGraph <code>thread_id</code> in the run's metadata becomes the session id, so every run of a thread is one session:</p>
    <Code title="python" code={langsmithCall} />
    <Code title="output" code={langsmithOut} />
  </details>

  <h2 id="claude-agent-sdk">Claude Agent SDK</h2>
  <p>The Agent SDK runs Claude Code, which exports its own telemetry when you pass these variables:</p>
  <Code code="npm install @anthropic-ai/claude-agent-sdk" />
  <Code title="claude-agent.mjs" code={claudeSdk} />
  <Code code={`node claude-agent.mjs "Label this issue: Viewer search misses tool output"\ncoding.sh sessions`} />
  <p>
    The run is recorded under the agent <code>claude-code</code>, titled with the prompt. In Python, <code>ClaudeAgentOptions(env=&#123;...&#125;)</code>
    takes the same keys. Don't use the console exporter under the SDK: it writes to the channel the SDK reads.
  </p>
  <Code title="output" code={claudeSdkOut} />
  <details class="more" id="claude-code">
    <summary>Claude Code in a terminal</summary>
    <p>
      <code>coding.sh init</code> records Claude Code with hooks, which needs nothing here (see <a href="/docs/agents">Coding agents</a>). To
      also send its telemetry, export these before you start it:
    </p>
    <Code code={claudeEnv} />
  </details>

  <h2 id="vercel-ai-sdk">Vercel AI SDK</h2>
  <p>
    The coding.sh SDK's <code>aiSdkTracer()</code> sends the AI SDK's spans with no OpenTelemetry setup. The complete example, for AI SDK 7
    and earlier versions, is in <a href="/docs/how-to/trace-your-agent#trace-the-vercel-ai-sdk">Trace your agent with the SDK</a>. An app
    that already registers a tracer provider can point its OTLP exporter at <code>http://127.0.0.1:4318/v1/traces</code> instead.
  </p>

  <h2 id="python">Any Python app</h2>
  <p>
    <code>coding_sh.otel.configure()</code> builds the tracer provider and the exporter. Any OpenTelemetry instrumentation then sends to
    coding.sh; here, OpenInference's for the OpenAI client:
  </p>
  <Code code={pyInstall} />
  <Code title="app.py" code={pyApp} />
  <Code title="output" code={pyOut} />
  <details class="more" id="env">
    <summary>Configure with environment variables instead</summary>
    <p>The OpenTelemetry SDK reads these. A signal-specific <code>OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_TRACES_ENDPOINT</code> is used as is, so include <code>/v1/traces</code>.</p>
    <Code code={otelEnv} />
    <p>The provider and exporter then need no arguments:</p>
    <Code title="app.py" code={envProvider} />
  </details>

  <h2 id="agent-runs">Vercel Agent Runs and eve</h2>
  <p>
    eve and durable-harness trace each agent turn for Vercel Agent Runs. Add the receiver as one more span processor, and each run is also
    a coding.sh session; Agent Runs keeps its copy.
  </p>
  <CodeTabs
    group="agent-runs"
    tabs={[
      { label: "durable-harness", title: "instrumentation.ts", code: harness },
      { label: "eve", title: "agent/instrumentation/coding-sh.ts", code: eve },
    ]}
  />
  <p>
    Each run (<code>agent.run.id</code>) is one session under the agent <code>eve</code> or <code>durable-harness</code>. A durable-harness turn
    with one tool call, in <code>coding.sh show wrun_demo</code>:
  </p>
  <Code title="output" code={agentRunsOut} />
  <p>
    A session's <code>traceAudience</code> decides what the spans record. With the default capture, <code>private</code> and
    <code>unknown</code> sessions record models, tools, timing and usage, but no prompts or replies.
  </p>

  <h2 id="grouping">Group traces into one session</h2>
  <p>
    Set <code>gen_ai.conversation.id</code> or <code>session.id</code> on the spans, and every trace with that id becomes a turn of one
    session. The AI SDK takes <code>metadata.sessionId</code>. With OpenInference, wrap the runs in <code>using_session</code>:
  </p>
  <Code title="python" code={grouping} />
  <p>Tag eval runs with <code>OTEL_RESOURCE_ATTRIBUTES=coding_sh.run_id=&lt;id&gt;</code>.</p>

  <details class="more" id="sessions">
    <summary>How spans become sessions</summary>
    <p>
      Model-call spans become <code>llm.call</code> events with duration, model and token usage. Tool spans become <code>tool.start</code> and
      <code>tool.end</code>. Nested agent spans become subagents with their own child session. Usage is read only from model-call spans,
      because agent spans usually repeat their children's totals.
    </p>
    <p>
      Spans arrive out of order, so after each batch the sessions they touch are rebuilt from all their events. Event ids come from the trace
      and span ids, so an exporter's retries store nothing twice.
    </p>
    <div class="doc-table">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Attribute</th><th>What it sets</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><code>gen_ai.usage.reasoning.output_tokens</code>, AI SDK <code>reasoningTokens</code>, Codex <code>reasoning_token_count</code>, Gemini CLI <code>thoughts_token_count</code></td>
            <td>The reasoning tokens of the model call. They are part of the output tokens, so totals do not change.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>gen_ai.skill.name</code> on a tool span</td>
            <td>The skill, with its source and resource. The session gets a <code>skill:&lt;name&gt;</code> tag.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>gen_ai.skill.script.exit_code</code></td>
            <td>The tool call's exit code.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Claude Code <code>skill_activated</code>, Codex <code>codex.skill_invocation</code></td>
            <td>The skill, recorded as invoked.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>gen_ai.conversation.compacted</code>, Claude Code <code>compaction</code></td>
            <td>A compaction event; Claude Code's also has the tokens before and after.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>gen_ai.main_agent.name</code> on the resource</td>
            <td>The agent name, ahead of <code>service.name</code>.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Claude Code <code>agent_id</code>, <code>parent_agent_id</code>; Codex <code>codex.agent_response</code></td>
            <td>Links a subagent's events to its parent.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </details>

  <h2 id="forward">Forward to another collector</h2>
  <p>
    Configured <a href="/docs/storage#sinks">sinks</a> get a copy of every event received over OTLP, in <code>collector</code> and
    <code>serve</code>. <code>coding.sh serve</code> can also forward every trace it stores to another OTLP collector: set
    <code>CODING_SH_FORWARD_URL</code>. See <a href="/docs/production">Trace an app in production</a>.
  </p>

